STANDARDS-COMPLIANCE READING (relevant to the second half of the directive): the repo's standards claims are currently shape-level, not conformance-level. manifest standards_profile (run.py:3794-3795) is a free-text string; MaReport (records.py:94-114) is TS-103-759-*shaped* but is extended with ad-hoc non-schema keys after to_dict() (run.py:2623-2642), detector_outputs carries only the top reason (run.py:2617-2618), and cert_validity is hardcoded {sig_valid,not_expired,not_revoked,chain_ok}=True (run.py:2619) even when sig_valid=False is passed. IEEE 1609.2 shows up only as HashedId8 (crypto_abstract.py:58-60) — there is no 1609.2 certificate structure, no ASN.1/COER encoding, and no signature is ever computed or verified on the dataset path (crypto_abstract.sign/verify are defined and never called; Vehicle.priv is None, Vehicle.pub is b""). CAMP SCP2 linkage (linkage.py) is the one component that is genuinely implemented to spec and load-bearing; CAMP SCP1 butterfly (butterfly.py) is spec-faithful but dead code, imported only by tests/test_butterfly.py. If "standards-compliant" is to become a checkable property rather than a label, the natural move is to make the record schemas and the crypto backend themselves the first two plugin points, with a conformance profile (e.g. "ieee1609.2+scms", "etsi-ts-102-941") selected in config, recorded in the manifest, and enforced by a validator in CI alongside the leakage linter.

